A||||14||Alex2004||Why don't the rear brakes EVER lock up?||||4||
Z||000000||Alex2004||12-29-2000||10:38 PM||||It just seems odd to me that the rear brakes on my 84 SE have never locked up. I mean, not that i drive all that often or anything but, the other day it had sleeted and snowed and i was driving the Fiero around in our garage driveway which is big and i was peeling out and stuff, and when i slammed on the brakes the front ones are the only ones that lock up, i even looked out the window to see if they locked up and they didn't. Is this normal? I wasn't going fast but still. And once when i got to drive on this dirt road with my uncle i was doing the same thing except i was going about 25 and slammed on the brakes seeing if they would lock up and they didn't. The road was wide so i wasn't in any danger! <P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||12-29-2000||10:48 PM||||Unless you happen to like spinning you don't want them to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||12-30-2000||12:38 PM||||Mine don't either.  I guess they did, once, when I was going about 30 and mashed them all the way to the floor board.  The only other way the rear ones lock is if you use the e-brake.  Not a good alternative, though.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex2004||12-30-2000||12:46 PM||||Oh, haha. I think they should lock up, like when your on asphalt when there is some gravel on it like at an intersection, you'd basically slid through it unless you got the rear brakes to lock. I just think it seems odd.  Every one of our vehicals lock up when mashed to the floor (EXCEPT our Dodge, it has ABS. But, i dunno. I still think they should lock up on ICE, maybe i should have went faster and tried.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||12-30-2000||01:13 PM||||Yeah, they obviously should lock up. However, I don't know if mine do. Well, they do if you really mash it to the floor. Maybe you just need a bit of bleeding?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-30-2000||01:54 PM||||GM puts a big safety margin in their braking systems. The rears are not designed to really lock-up, mine do if I literally stand on the pedal. The wheel(s)with the least grip want to lead. If the rears lockup and the fronts don't, they have the least grip, and therefore want to be in front, causing a spin. GM tries to make sure the rears won't lockup in all conditions.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex2004||12-30-2000||02:50 PM||||We will probably bleed the brakes whenever it warms up.  But thanks for the info!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Alex...<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Scott_M||12-30-2000||11:45 PM||||My rears locked up when I hit the off ramp going too fast on a rainy day. The rear end spun out maybe 45 degrees before I got control. Luckly it was almost 5 am and no one was around.||||reg||4
Z||000008||rodmcneill||12-31-2000||01:37 AM||||You're rear brakes may not be working properly, remember that when you use your handbrake, that is what adjusts your rear calipers. If you feel that the brakes are not balanced properly(the fronts are doing most of the work) then it is time to check out the rear rotors for thickness and brake pads also. Make sure your hand brake cables are not rusted up. Check your rear calipers to make sure they are not frozen up or leaking.||||reg||1
Z||000009||tiacPaul||12-31-2000||04:11 AM||||I quetion my rear brakes too.  They got new pads and a new caliper.  I also had to do a new p-brake lever.  My parking brake still won't grab well enough to keep the car from moving on a slight hill.  I also tried to adjust the brakes by applying the p-brake over and over, still no decent brake in back.  But the lever is moving both calipers.  ANY  ideas????  ||||reg||5
Z||000010||FieroJoe||12-31-2000||06:11 PM||||tiacPaul -<BR>  Have you checked your e-brake equalizer, the thing that connects the cables, near the tailpipe?  This is your adjustment for the e-brake tension, as the cables will stretch after a while.  After about 7-8 clicks of the  ebrake handle, your car shouldnt move at all.  If the screw on the equalizer is at the left most extreme (i think its left), then the cables are as tight as they can be, unless there is some other way to tension the cables more...?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex2004||12-31-2000||09:01 PM||||Well i went outside just to mess with the car and thought about whatyuo guys have said, and i pulled the parking brake and it works great, and then when i put it down...The rear brakes work better!?! The brake petal is tighter, i haven't got them to fully lock yet because i was in the garage but you can tell it works better. Why do you think it does that though? Does the parking brake thing need to be adjusted?<BR>Let me know, thanks...<BR>Alex<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A> ||||reg||7
Z||000012||rodmcneill||12-31-2000||10:23 PM||||On the front brakes they are self adjusting for wear on the pads. The rear brakes are not self adjusting. Using the parking brake is what adjusts brake pad to rotor clearance. Remember to push down on the brake pedal when applying the parking brake. I think a lot of owners of automatic Fiero's don't use their parking brakes, this can lead to the cables rusting up and rear brakes barely working. This puts a lot more load on the front brakes and the Fiero needs as much help as it can get in the braking dept.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex2004||12-31-2000||11:13 PM||||OK, thank's. We replaced the parking brake cable. So i guess i'll just adjust the brakes like 2-3 times a month or so. Thanks!<BR>Alex...<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000014||Alex2004||01-01-2001||06:03 PM||||Just checking something!<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A>  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.linkline.com/personal/lvumlow/car_cornering.gif"> ||||reg||10
